:expert_capacity a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Expert Capacity"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasCQ "CQ_S0_R1_0014"^^xsd:string,
        "CQ_S0_R1_0040"^^xsd:string,
        "CQ_S0_R1_0099"^^xsd:string,
        "CQ_S0_R1_0188"^^xsd:string,
        "CQ_S0_R1_0203"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasEvidenceChunk "2503.05066_rag::chunk_009"^^xsd:string,
        "2504.19925_rag::chunk_005"^^xsd:string,
        "2504.19925_rag::chunk_012"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasEvidenceText "current systems impose a fixed capacity limit on the number of tokens that can be routed to each expert class. Expert capacity aims to avoid long iterations"^^xsd:string,
        "expert capacity , which defines the maximum number of tokens each expert class can process"^^xsd:string,
        "expert capacity enforces MoE layers to discard overflowed tokens"^^xsd:string,
        "setting an expert capacity , which defines the maximum number of tokens each expert class can process"^^xsd:string ;
    rdfs:subClassOf :entity,
        owl:Thing ;
    skos:skos_definition "The limit enforced on MoE layers that determines the maximum number of tokens an expert can handle, beyond which tokens are dropped."^^xsd:string ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom "CQ_S0_R1_0014"^^xsd:string,
        "CQ_S0_R1_0040"^^xsd:string,
        "CQ_S0_R1_0099"^^xsd:string,
        "CQ_S0_R1_0188"^^xsd:string,
        "CQ_S0_R1_0203"^^xsd:string .

:moe_system a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "MoE System"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasCQ "CQ_S0_R1_0016"^^xsd:string,
        "CQ_S0_R1_0026"^^xsd:string,
        "CQ_S0_R1_0032"^^xsd:string,
        "CQ_S0_R1_0047"^^xsd:string,
        "CQ_S0_R1_0085"^^xsd:string,
        "CQ_S0_R1_0122"^^xsd:string,
        "CQ_S0_R1_0134"^^xsd:string,
        "CQ_S0_R1_0181"^^xsd:string,
        "CQ_S0_R1_0208"^^xsd:string,
        "CQ_S0_R1_0233"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasEvidenceChunk "1qP3lsatCR_rag::chunk_004"^^xsd:string,
        "2304.11414_rag::chunk_006"^^xsd:string,
        "2401.13920_rag::chunk_001"^^xsd:string,
        "2408.04307_rag::chunk_004"^^xsd:string,
        "2411.10003_rag::chunk_000"^^xsd:string,
        "2412.07067_rag::chunk_001"^^xsd:string,
        "2504.05586_rag::chunk_003"^^xsd:string,
        "9CqkpQExe2_rag::chunk_001"^^xsd:string,
        "bjFhVbky5A_rag::chunk_001"^^xsd:string ;
    :hasEvidenceText "In each MoE layer, each token will be routed by the gating network to top K different experts for processing"^^xsd:string,
        "Mixture of Expert (MoE) enables a nearly constant computational budget as model scaling. Generally, we replace some layers of a foundation model with MoE ones to generate a MoE model."^^xsd:string,
        "Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architecture has been recently gaining enormous attention for the scaling up of LLMs while maintaining roughly constant FLOPs."^^xsd:string,
        "Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architecture has been recently gaining enormous attention for the scaling up of LLMs while maintaining roughly constant FLOPs. By incorporating multiple expert networks and employing a sparse gating mechanism"^^xsd:string,
        "MoE layer, including Expert(1- 0, 1-1, 1-2), are in the 1 transformer layer."^^xsd:string,
        "MoE-CAP introduces new sparsity-aware metrics and CAP radar diagrams to accurately and comprehensively evaluate MoE systems"^^xsd:string,
        "The Mixtures-of-Experts (MoE) model is a widespread distributed and integrated learning method for large language models (LLM)"^^xsd:string,
        "The core of the MoE architecture comprises a set of expert networks governed by a routing strategy."^^xsd:string,
        "The parallel architecture with bound DP and EP of existing MoE models is the key bottleneck of training efciency ."^^xsd:string,
        "modern MoE systems increasingly offload experts to DRAM and SSDs [49, 13] and delegate part of the computation to CPUs"^^xsd:string,
        "the sparse mixture-of-experts (MoE) architecture is often adopted, which comprises a sparse gate network and a series of expert networks."^^xsd:string ;
    rdfs:subClassOf :entity,
        owl:Thing ;
    skos:altLabel "MoE system"^^xsd:string ;
    skos:skos_definition "A system based on the Mixture-of-Experts architecture, which incorporates multiple expert networks and a sparse gating mechanism to enable efficient computation and scaling of models."^^xsd:string ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom "CQ_S0_R1_0016"^^xsd:string,
        "CQ_S0_R1_0026"^^xsd:string,
        "CQ_S0_R1_0032"^^xsd:string,
        "CQ_S0_R1_0047"^^xsd:string,
        "CQ_S0_R1_0085"^^xsd:string,
        "CQ_S0_R1_0122"^^xsd:string,
        "CQ_S0_R1_0134"^^xsd:string,
        "CQ_S0_R1_0181"^^xsd:string,
        "CQ_S0_R1_0208"^^xsd:string,
        "CQ_S0_R1_0233"^^xsd:string .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2201_05596_rag__chunk_001> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2201.05596_rag::chunk_001" ;
    kg:docId "2201.05596_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2201_05596_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "reduce the compute requirements to train large models without sacricing model quality. To this end, architectures based on Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) [ 3 , 4 , 5 ] have paved a promising path, enabling sub-linear compute requirements with respect to the model parameters and allowing for improved model quality without increasing training cost. However, MoE based models have their own set of challenges that limit their use in a wide range of real world scenarios: Limited Scope The scope of MoE based" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2205_10034_rag__chunk_001> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2205.10034_rag::chunk_001" ;
    kg:docId "2205.10034_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2205_10034_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "differ from densely activated models but require significant computational resources to surpass existing benchmarks. To address these challenges, Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) based sparsely activated neural networks have been introduced for training larger models with minimal or no additional computational resources, while still achieving improved training outcomes [23][26]. MoE architectures activate only a subset of parameters based on the input data, unlike densely activated models. This selectiv" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2205_10034_rag__chunk_012> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2205.10034_rag::chunk_012" ;
    kg:docId "2205.10034_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2205_10034_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "6c). These elastic training approaches effectively mitigate performance degradation resulting from load imbalance. You can find detailed performance comparisons in Section 5.3. In elastic training, load imbalance often leads to situations where some devices are idle, waiting for others to complete their computations, creating what is known as \"bubbles.\" These bubbles not only reduce computational efficiency but also increase training costs. To address this, we have introduced a method of scaling" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2303_06182_rag__chunk_003> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2303.06182_rag::chunk_003" ;
    kg:docId "2303.06182_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2303_06182_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "to the normal dense Transformer layer, it introduces a new kind of layer with sparse MoEs. Sparse MoE layers replace the FFN block with an MoE block that consists of multiple different expert FFNs. Instead of applying a single FFN to all the input tokens, it first uses a gating function to decide which expert(s) is most suitable for each token, and then routes the tokens to their corresponding expert. Typically, a token is routed to one or two experts in a policy that is referred to as top-1 or " .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2303_06182_rag__chunk_004> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2303.06182_rag::chunk_004" ;
    kg:docId "2303.06182_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2303_06182_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "of MoE Transformer models, we study two major use cases: Language Modeling and Machine Translation. Language modeling generates the probability an input sequence appears in natural text whereas machine translation maps the input from one language to another. Both tasks are core problems to natural language processing, and are currently major applications of MoE Transformers. We choose models in recent publications that achieved state-of-the-art as our testbed. The details of the datasets and mod" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2303_06182_rag__chunk_005> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2303.06182_rag::chunk_005" ;
    kg:docId "2303.06182_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2303_06182_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "requires more memory for activation. (results for batchsize=48 for MT, and batchsize=8 for LM. Note that these are the largest batch sizes that are feasible to run under the baseline implementation.) GPU 8 NVIDIA Tesla V100, with 5120 CUDA cores, 32GB HBM2 memory at 900GB/s connected by NVLink at 300GB/s TABLE I E XPERIMENTAL S ETUP . LM: L ANGUAGE M ODELING . MT: M ACHINE T RANSLATION . E: NUMBER OF EXPERTS . MF: M O E L AYER F REQUENCY . CF: C APACITY F ACTOR . TD: T OKEN D IMENSION . HD: H ID" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2304_11414_rag__chunk_009> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2304.11414_rag::chunk_009" ;
    kg:docId "2304.11414_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2304_11414_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "al. (2022), and barely applied in MoE. The reason is two folds. On one hand, existing DPMoE frameworks usually involve data parallel, expert parallel, and tensor parallel, composing a complex parallel system that requires a lot of engineering effort. Combining such a complex scheme with pipeline parallel is an even further complicated task 5 . On the other hand, with a large number of experts, the combination of data parallel, expert parallel, and tensor parallel is already able to scale models " .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2308_12066_rag__chunk_013> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2308.12066_rag::chunk_013" ;
    kg:docId "2308.12066_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2308_12066_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "the active expert parameters over two consecutive (the N -th and ( N +1)-th) MoE blocks. Since expert parameters account for the majority of MoE-based LLMs model size (see Figure 3) and only a small fraction of experts are activated during inference, the peak GPU memory usage in Equation 1 becomes much lower than GPU-only and can also reach the memory consumption level of the memory-optimal MoE-OnDemand design. A key advantage of reducing peak GPU memory usage is that it facilitates the deployme" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2401_08383_rag__chunk_003> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2401.08383_rag::chunk_003" ;
    kg:docId "2401.08383_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2401_08383_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "expert selection is not random and routing decisions in previous layers will largely affect the later 1) We exploit the expert affinity property that exists implicitly in current pre-trained GPT MoE models by capturing the combined conditional probability of the expert routing decisions across multiple MoE layers. 2) We thoroughly analyze the computation and communication patterns in existing parallelism strategies, and propose a context-coherent expert parallelism to largely reduce the number o" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2401_08383_rag__chunk_012> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2401.08383_rag::chunk_012" ;
    kg:docId "2401.08383_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2401_08383_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "7 B. Reducing Collective Communications with ContextCoherent Expert Parallelism In the vanilla expert parallelism, each MoE layer strictly requires two Alltoall collectives because tokens need to be gathered by their corresponding GPU in the data-parallel group to perform the attention computation. With our contextcoherent design, however, contexts of all tokens are coherent and visible on every GPU, meaning that we no longer need the second Alltoall to retrieve tokens, instead, they can perform" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2401_08383_rag__chunk_016> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2401.08383_rag::chunk_016" ;
    kg:docId "2401.08383_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2401_08383_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "and it keeps increasing as experts become more domain-specific, thus the affinity gets more salient among experts at different layers. [TABLE id=2401.08383_p10_tIII] caption: Table III shows the expert affinity on datasets that are not included in the training data. columns: col1 | col2 row: col1=VI. R ELATED; col2=W ORK [/TABLE] G. How Many Tokens Are Needed to Capture the Expert Affinity in a Pre-Trained Model? In V-A , we briefly mentioned how to properly solve the integer linear programming " .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2401_13920_rag__chunk_005> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2401.13920_rag::chunk_005" ;
    kg:docId "2401.13920_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2401_13920_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "Transformers Similar to the classic MoE skeleton applied to Transformer structures such as GShard, the MoE layer in our model mainly consists of a MSA layer, a gating network, a routing module, and several expert FFNs. The output of the MoE layer can be depicted as follows: T T where n denotes the number of experts, denotes the batch containing T tokens. f i denotes the proportion of tokens assigned to expert i , and P i denotes the average probability that the router chooses expert i . The auxi" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2402_07033_rag__chunk_006> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2402.07033_rag::chunk_006" ;
    kg:docId "2402.07033_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2402_07033_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "3 b). When some expert weights are missing on the GPU memory ( 1 ), they are copied from the CPU memory to the GPU memory ( 2 ), and then the GPU executes the expert layer ( 3 ). Existing offloading systems use this method. Another approach is to copy the activations from the GPU memory to CPU memory and execute the expert layer on the CPU (Figure 3 c). In this approach, when some expert weights are missing on the GPU memory ( 1 ), the activation values are copied from the GPU memory to the CPU " .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2403_07816_rag__chunk_012> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2403.07816_rag::chunk_012" ;
    kg:docId "2403.07816_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2403_07816_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "50.0 34.7 no load-balancing (LB) 34.6 19.5 23.2 44.4 51.6 34.6 no LB & freeze experts 34.8 18.3 24.1 44.9 51.4 34.7 blending experts 13.9 17.1 9.9 34.1 36.2 22.2 split experts, top-2 of 8 22.0 20.1 16.8 39.1 41.8 28.0 split experts, top-4 of 8 29.6 26.8 22.9 44.0 49.4 34.5 [TABLE id=2403.07816_p9_t5] caption: Table 5 Ablations on different BTX training strategies. All variants are initialized from the same experts and trained for a total of 10B tokens during MoE finetuning. columns: gsm8k | huma" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2404_05019_rag__chunk_003> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2404.05019_rag::chunk_003" ;
    kg:docId "2404.05019_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2404_05019_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan ") , k )) , (2) ( H ( x ) i , if H ( x ) i TopK ( H ( x )) . , otherwise. (3) H ( x ) i = ( x W gate ) i + i , (4) TopK ( H ( x ) , k ) i = i = StandardNormal () Softplus (( x W noise ) i ) , (5) where is tunable Gaussian noise, W gate and W noise denote two trainable weight matrices. Leveraging sparse output of G ( x ) , this approach significantly increases the number of model parameters without 2 + Outputs + Outputs Outputs 1 Outputs n + + All-to-All Combine Decode Decode E2 E1 E4 E3 E1 SE E3 " .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2404_05019_rag__chunk_007> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2404.05019_rag::chunk_007" ;
    kg:docId "2404.05019_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2404_05019_rag> ;
