<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1qP3lsatCR_rag__chunk_002> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "1qP3lsatCR_rag::chunk_002" ;
    kg:docId "1qP3lsatCR_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1qP3lsatCR_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "on the routing result of the gating network, each token is sent to the device where the selected expert is located. The output from the expert is then sent back to the original device of the corresponding token. This involves two communication operations, namely the All-to-All scatter and All-to-All gather (He et al., 2021), respectively. Due to the dynamic nature of routing, training MoE models efficiently faces several challenges, with the All-to-All communication, being the most significant o" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2201_05596_rag__chunk_017> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2201.05596_rag::chunk_017" ;
    kg:docId "2201.05596_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2201_05596_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "large as the entire set of parameters, making it challenging to achieve both low latency and high throughput. To address this issue, we partition experts across devices, group all input tokens assigned to the same experts under the same critical data path, and parallelize processing of the token groups with dierent critical paths among dierent devices using expert parallelism. In the example of 1.3B+MoE-128, when expert parallelism is equal to 128, each GPU only processes a single token group co" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2303_06182_rag__chunk_006> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2303.06182_rag::chunk_006" ;
    kg:docId "2303.06182_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2303_06182_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "peak, an increase of 8.58 . For MT, the dense and MoE models use 7.02GB and 21.16GB, respectively, an increase of 3.01 . We perform a detailed analysis by separating static and dynamic memory usage. Static memory consumption refers to memory allocated to model parameters, whereas dynamic memory consumption refers to memory allocated on demand, usually by network activations. Due to the fact that each GPU accommodates more than one expert during inference, the increase in static memory is expecte" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2303_06182_rag__chunk_011> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2303.06182_rag::chunk_011" ;
    kg:docId "2303.06182_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2303_06182_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "the sizes of dispatched input are variable [13], we adopt a two-step approach. First, we use an all-to-all collective to inform each device the size of the incoming tokens. Second, we use another all-to-all collective to perform the real token transfer. The size all-to-all collective is launched as soon as sizes are known, maximizing overlap with other kernels. Meanwhile, inputs are reordered based on the optimal index for each token and the reordered input will be split based on the size of inp" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2304_11414_rag__chunk_006> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2304.11414_rag::chunk_006" ;
    kg:docId "2304.11414_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2304_11414_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "this subsection, we showcase the parallel architecture of existing MoE systems and analyze its advantages and drawbacks in terms of training efciency in a systematic view. Two important conclusions can be drawn: (1) The parallel architecture with bound DP and EP of existing MoE models is the key bottleneck of training efciency . (2) Current parallel architecture hinders the integration of TP and PP into existing MoE models, especially TP. In the rest of this paper, we call the MoE with this typi" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2401_13920_rag__chunk_001> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2401.13920_rag::chunk_001" ;
    kg:docId "2401.13920_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2401_13920_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "and we make the following optimizaThe Mixtures-of-Experts (MoE) model is a widespread distributed and integrated learning method for large language models (LLM), which is favored due to its ability to sparsify and expand models efficiently. However, the performance of MoE is limited by load imbalance and high latency of All-to-All communication, along with relatively redundant computation owing to large expert capacity. Load imbalance may result from existing routing policies that consistently t" .

